.TH CRAM 1 "November 2026" "cram 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cram \- track your study hours from the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cram
.br
.B cram
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cram
keeps a log of the time you study and what you covered in each session,
with totals per day and per week.
.PP
Run
.B cram
on its own in a terminal to open the full-screen app.
Every action is also available as a
.B cram
.I command
for scripts and quick use from the shell.
When output is not a terminal, plain
.B cram
prints the dashboard instead.
.PP
Everything is written to disk the moment it changes.
Weeks run Monday to Sunday; a session that runs past midnight counts on the day it started.
.SH THE APP
Type into the prompt bar at the bottom:
.TP
.BR in " [\fIclass\fR] [\fInote\fR]"
Start studying. The words after \fBin\fR are matched against your classes:
the full name (\fBin linear algebra\fR), the start of it (\fBin linear\fR) or its initials (\fBin la\fR).
Anything after the class becomes the first note. If nothing matches you are asked whether to add it as a class.
.TP
.B out
Stop. Shows what you covered, asks "Did you do anything else not listed?",
lets you adjust the day and times, then saves the session.
.TP
.BR undo " [\fIn\fR], " redo " [\fIn\fR]"
Take back the last change (or put it back).
.TP
.BR /vday " [\fIday\fR]"
View a day: its sessions, total and a timeline bar. Enter on a session edits it;
the last row, \fB+ add a session\fR, adds one.
.TP
.BR /vweek " [\fIweek\fR]"
View a week's sessions with a timeline per day.
.TP
.BR /weeks
A calendar of all your weeks. Up/down picks one;
Enter opens its log.
.TP
.B /classes
Add or remove your classes, with hours this week and in total.
.TP
.B /config
Color theme.
.TP
.B /help
All commands and keys.
.PP
Typing \fB/\fR opens a menu of these; arrow keys pick one, Tab completes, Enter runs it.
What you cover goes in the separate \fInotes\fR box.
.PP
If a session's times overlap another session you are offered to merge them, fit the new session
into the free gap, shorten the other session, or replace it.
.SS Keys
.TP 18
.B tab
Switch between the prompt bar and the top section.
.TP
.B arrows
In the top section, move between the notes box, its notes, the today panel
and the week panel. Enter on a panel opens it.
.TP
.B ctrl+s
Start studying / stop.
.TP
.B ctrl+n
Jump to the notes box.
.TP
.BR ctrl+z ", " ctrl+y
Undo, redo.
.TP
.B ctrl+p
Command palette (past sessions, themes, ...).
.TP
.B ctrl+t
Fix the last session's times.
.TP
.B ctrl+l
Week log.
.TP
.B ctrl+b
Show or hide the side panels.
.TP
.BR f2 ", " delete
Rename or remove the selected note.
.TP
.B f1
All shortcuts.
.TP
.B ctrl+q
Quit. Always asks first; when you are studying you can stop or keep the session running.
.SH COMMANDS
Week arguments (\fB\-w\fR) accept \fBthis\fR, \fBlast\fR, \fB\-2\fR, or any date inside the week.
Days accept \fBtoday\fR, \fByesterday\fR, weekday names, \fB10/14\fR, \fB10/14/26\fR or \fB2026\-10\-14\fR.
Times accept \fB9\fR, \fB9am\fR, \fB9:30pm\fR, \fB930pm\fR, \fB21:30\fR, \fBnoon\fR and \fBmidnight\fR.
.TP
.BR in " [\fIclass\fR] [\fInote\fR...] [\fB\-\-at\fR \fItime\fR] [\fB\-c\fR \fIclass\fR]"
Start studying. The class is matched the same way as in the app; \fB\-c\fR names it explicitly.
An ambiguous short name (\fBchem\fR for both Chemistry and Chem Lab) is refused.
.TP
.BR out " [\fInote\fR...] [\fB\-\-at\fR \fItime\fR]"
Stop studying. Without a note it shows the session and asks for anything missing.
.TP
.BR add " [\fItext\fR...]"
Describe what you covered in the current session. Without text it asks for lines.
When you're not studying, it asks whether to add to an earlier session, then lets you pick one from today or
another day this week.
.TP
.BR edit " [\fB\-c\fR \fIclass\fR] [\fB\-\-date\fR] [\fB\-\-start\fR] [\fB\-\-end\fR] [\fB\-\-note\fR]... [\fB\-\-add\-note\fR]..."
Change a session. Lets you pick one from today or another day this week, then choose to edit its time, what
you covered, or its class, in place. A session from today keeps its date. If the new times overlap another session, it offers to
merge them, fit this one into the gap, shorten the other, replace it, or change the times again.
.TP
.B v\-block
Show the session you are in, or your most recent one.
.TP
.BR v\-today " [\fIday\fR]"
Show a day's timeline and its sessions. Defaults to today.
.TP
.BR v\-week " [\fB\-w\fR \fIweek\fR]"
Show hours per day, every session and the totals for a week.
.TP
.BR status " [\fB\-W\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIweek\fR]"
Studying or not, hours studied today, and the week; \fB\-W\fR keeps it live.
.TP
.BR class " [\fBadd\fR \fIname\fR[, \fIname\fR...] | \fBrm\fR \fIname\fR | \fBrename\fR \fIold\fR \fInew\fR]"
Manage your classes. On its own (or as \fBcram classes\fR) it lists them with hours this week, in total,
and when you last studied each. Removing a class keeps it on past sessions, so no hours are lost.
.TP
.BR weeks " [\fB\-n\fR \fIcount\fR]"
Recent weeks with hours studied and days studied.
.TP
.BR undo " [\fIn\fR], " redo " [\fIn\fR]"
Undo or redo changes. The last 100 are kept.
.TP
.BR config " [\fB\-e\fR] [\fB\-\-path\fR]"
Show the config, open it in \fB$VISUAL\fR/\fB$EDITOR\fR, or print where files live.
.TP
.B cycle\-theme
Switch to the next color theme.
.SH CONFIGURATION
\fI~/.config/cram/config.toml\fR is created on first run. Sections:
.TP
.B [ui]
theme (\fBcatppuccin\fR, \fBnord\fR, \fBgruvbox\fR, \fBtokyonight\fR, \fBrosepine\fR, \fBleonida\fR),
banner and mascot (\fBtrue\fR/\fBfalse\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/cram/config.toml
Theme, banner and mascot settings.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/cram/data.json
The log. \fIdata.json.bak\fR is the previous version; a damaged file is replaced by it automatically.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/cram/undo/ ", " redo/
Undo history.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/cram/session.json
Heartbeat used to notice that cram closed unexpectedly while you were studying.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CRAM_HOME
Keep config, data and cache in this one folder instead (handy for testing).
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME
Respected when \fBCRAM_HOME\fR is not set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when a command is refused (for example starting twice), 2 when an argument
cannot be read (for example a time like \fB25pm\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cram in linear algebra
cram add "eigenvectors, ch. 5"
cram out --at 5:15pm
cram v-week -w last
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/ricozama121803/cram
